      "aiApproved": "yes",
      "aiApprovedAssetSha256": "sha256:753d52c87cc429977f9b2cc3372f6a4e7713fbaf92455e2343667ec612514811",
      "aiReviewedAt": "2026-10-08",
      "aiReviewer": "OpenAI Codex economics_visual_memory_audit",
      "aiNotes": "Actual independent image review: curricula/DE/Gymnasium/quality/goal-visualization-review/wirtschaft-e2-twenty-image-author-20261008-v1/independent-review/af709beb-2a7e-5df0-bc91-f8f0e0cb99f8/independent-image-review.receipt.json (sha256:a13e6a7e7f52d580c66e95eaa8f0b53035fda79330bdf9c6bfad6606eeca97c9). A competition authority investigates a market-entry obstacle and weighs access against production and innovation. The scene prompts instrument selection and effects evaluation; it asserts neither automatic breakup nor universal harmfulness of large firms. The literal wall is a transparent abstraction of exclusion, not a claim about a current legal case. At 360 px the blocked entrance, customer, investigator, scale, door and gear symbols carry the central decision. The small checklist is supplementary and not needed to identify the problem and trade-off. At 680 px the access/production-and-innovation balance and optional investigation checklist can be read; both shops and the authority remain distinct. The investigator looks through a magnifier toward the barrier; her closed authority folder is an identifying cover rather than a page she reads. Public shop notices are correctly customer-facing."
